e import. Choosing another engine means changing only how the transcriber is created. The options include NVIDIA's Parakeet models, which cover 25 European languages in v3, and Alibaba's Qwen3-ASR, which covers 30 languages and 22 Chinese dialects but is slower and better suited to files than live input. The package also adds speaker diarization, which Apple's framework lacks, using NVIDIA's Sortformer to track up to four speakers.\n\nThe authors' own measurements, on an M4 Max Mac Studio running macOS 27.0.1, give a sense of the gap. On LibriSpeech files of 3 to 8 minutes, Parakeet v3 had word error rates of 1.55% on test-clean and 2.78% on test-other, against 2.12% and 3.97% for Apple's transcriber. Meetings were harder: on AMI recordings with overlapping speech, every engine tested had an error rate above 23%. The authors note that the results come from one machine and a few datasets, and that they have not yet tested on an iPhone.\n\nAnyDecisionModel handles questions such as which team should take a support ticket. It reads the model's probabilities for each allowed answer instead of parsing generated text, so a result arrives as a typed value with a probability per option. The authors caution that raw language-model probabilities are often overconfident, and that the confidence value reflects how concentrated the probabilities are, which does not show whether an answer is correct. They recommend measuring error rates on your own data before choosing a threshold.","ttft_ms":5818,"type":"result","duration_ms":9731,"uuid":"f52e9261-366e-49f9-b8d9-d08973528455","ttft_stream_ms":834,"time_to_request_ms":68,"first_content_frame_ms":834,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
